clean_summary:
NVIDIA has now open sourced their trtllmgen MoE kernels! It's great to see parts of NVIDIA moving towards open kernels, as open source drives innovation. Now it's time for SemiAnalysis to encourage NVIDIA to open source their trtllmgen attention kernels too.

@cudagdb: "Trtllmgen kernels are now open. Fastest prefill and decode kernels for our target workloads. We wrote these to win InferenceX, MLPerf, and other benchmarks, powering some of today’s top served models. Dive in, learn, use them, or level up your own. Enjoy."

The linked GitHub repository contains the kernel library for LLM serving, allowing developers to contribute and enhance their projects.

Day-0 support for @deepseek_ai V4 Pro and Flash on vLLM has been announced, introducing a new generation of DeepSeek models designed for tasks up to 1M tokens. A first-principles walkthrough of the new long-context attention mechanism is being published alongside the release.

The attention mechanism includes shared K/V with inverse RoPE for 2× memory savings, c4a/c128a KV compression for 4×–128× savings, DeepSeek Sparse Attention over compressed tokens, and a short sliding window for locality across compression boundaries. At 1M context, the per-layer KV state is approximately 8.7× smaller than a DeepSeek V3.2-style 61-layer stack. vLLM features a unified hybrid KV cache and disaggregated serving for optimal performance.

@deepseek_ai: "DeepSeek-V4 Preview is officially live & open-sourced! Welcome to the era of cost-effective 1M context length. DeepSeek-V4-Pro has 1.6T total/49B active params, while DeepSeek-V4-Flash offers 284B total/13B active params. Try it now at via Expert Mode/Instant Mode."

Great reinforcement learning systems deep dive by @SemiAnalysis. Scaling RL is as much an infrastructure problem as an algorithm one.

SemiAnalysis ran experiments on our stack: Prime RL plus Sandboxes. System efficiency ultimately depends on queue health to match generator and trainer throughput.

@SemiAnalysis: "RL Systems Mind the Gap: Matching Trainer and Generator Throughput" covers RL training infrastructure topics such as GRPO, PipelineRL, Async RL, policy staleness, RL sandbox infrastructure, CPU requirements, TCO analysis, and the work of Thinking Machines Tinker.
